/// <h1>IosNotificationManager</h1>
/// Central entry point for local notification operations.
/// <h2>Overview</h2>
/// <ul>
///   <li>
///     Provides a singleton (<code>shared</code>) for global access.
///   </li>
///   <li>
///     <code>installDelegate()</code> installs the library’s <code>UNUserNotificationCenterDelegate</code>, which
///     presents the library’s notifications in the foreground and receives the responses to them,
///     and forwards the other notifications to the delegate that was set before it. Call it on the
///     main thread at launch, before <code>application(_:didFinishLaunchingWithOptions:)</code> returns (or
///     inside <code>scene(_:willConnectTo:options:)</code>; in a SwiftUI app, in <code>App.init</code> or in the
///     <code>UIApplicationDelegateAdaptor</code>’s <code>application(_:didFinishLaunchingWithOptions:)</code>). The
///     manager itself is not a <code>UNUserNotificationCenterDelegate</code>.
///   </li>
///   <li>
///     <code>responses</code> delivers the responses to the library’s notifications (taps, actions, replies,
///     the custom dismiss action) and <code>settingsRequests</code> the system’s requests to show the app’s
///     notification settings. Each keeps up to 32 events while it has no listener, so a listener
///     added after launch still receives the response that launched the app.
///     <code>launchResponse()</code> returns that response.
///   </li>
///   <li>
///     The library marks the notifications it adds with a reserved userInfo key
///     (<code>com.jonghyunkim.nativetoolkit</code>) to tell them from other components’ notifications; it
///     rejects a userInfo that uses the key and removes the key from every userInfo it hands back.
///   </li>
///   <li>
///     Every operation goes through its use case. The operations that complete have a callback
///     form (the existing <code>(isSuccess, errorMessage)</code> forms, whose <code>errorMessage</code> is the fixed
///     English description of a <code>NotificationError</code>) and an <code>async</code> form. The <code>async</code> forms are
///     declared <code>async throws</code> without a thrown type; the library’s repository throws a
///     <code>NotificationError</code> (a repository of your own may throw its own errors). The queries and
///     the categories do not throw.
///   </li>
///   <li>
///     <code>requestPermission(options:presenter:completion:)</code> and
///     <code>openNotificationSettings(presenter:completion:)</code> take a <code>PresenterSelection</code>:
///     <code>.foreground</code> (what the C ABI passes) fails with <code>NotificationError/notForeground</code> instead
///     of bringing up system UI while the app is not in the foreground.
///   </li>
///   <li>
///     <code>registerCategory(_:completion:)</code> and <code>removeCategory(identifier:completion:)</code> queue the
///     change inside the call; the changes take effect in the order of the calls, and the
///     completion follows the change.
///   </li>
/// </ul>
